1
00:00:02,125 --> 00:00:04,116
TESS: Couldn't sleep either?

2
00:00:04,136 --> 00:00:06,727
PIP: Your suitcase is by the door.

3
00:00:06,747 --> 00:00:07,776
TESS: Yeah.

4
00:00:07,796 --> 00:00:09,901
TESS: My bus leaves at seven.

5
00:00:10,912 --> 00:00:12,079
PIP: Tess?

6
00:00:12,099 --> 00:00:13,803
PIP: What if the Earth shrank?

7
00:00:13,823 --> 00:00:14,666
TESS: Shrank?

8
00:00:14,686 --> 00:00:15,629
PIP: Everything.

9
00:00:15,649 --> 00:00:17,646
PIP: The ground, the oceans, the air,

10
00:00:17,666 --> 00:00:18,935
PIP: even the Moon.

11
00:00:18,955 --> 00:00:20,260
PIP: Smaller and smaller.

12
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:21,879
PIP: But just as heavy.

13
00:00:21,899 --> 00:00:23,038
TESS: Same mass.

14
00:00:23,058 --> 00:00:24,671
TESS: Less space.

15
00:00:24,691 --> 00:00:26,117
TESS: Why?

16
00:00:26,137 --> 00:00:28,842
PIP: Then your school wouldn't be so far away.

17
00:00:29,615 --> 00:00:30,660
TESS: Okay.

18
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:32,410
TESS: Let's squeeze the world.

19
00:00:33,118 --> 00:00:36,531
NARRATOR: Imagine a bubble around the Earth and the Moon.

20
00:00:36,551 --> 00:00:38,731
NARRATOR: Everything inside it begins to shrink,

21
00:00:38,751 --> 00:00:41,262
NARRATOR: all in perfect proportion.

22
00:00:41,282 --> 00:00:42,747
NARRATOR: Every ocean.

23
00:00:42,767 --> 00:00:44,104
NARRATOR: Every cloud.

24
00:00:44,124 --> 00:00:45,976
NARRATOR: Every person.

25
00:00:45,996 --> 00:00:48,626
NARRATOR: But not a single gram is lost.

26
00:00:49,286 --> 00:00:50,718
TESS: Here's the weird part.

27
00:00:50,739 --> 00:00:52,459
TESS: You wouldn't even see it happen.

28
00:00:52,479 --> 00:00:53,365
PIP: Why not?

29
00:00:53,385 --> 00:00:54,885
TESS: Because you'd shrink too.

30
00:00:54,905 --> 00:00:56,708
TESS: Your ruler, your house, your eyes.

31
00:00:56,728 --> 00:00:58,275
TESS: Everything, together.

32
00:00:58,295 --> 00:01:01,417
TESS: The Moon would look exactly the same size.

33
00:01:01,438 --> 00:01:02,498
PIP: So...

34
00:01:02,518 --> 00:01:04,845
PIP: you wouldn't be any closer.

35
00:01:04,865 --> 00:01:06,667
TESS: Not to you.

36
00:01:06,687 --> 00:01:07,842
TESS: No.

37
00:01:08,925 --> 00:01:11,026
NARRATOR: But you would feel it.

38
00:01:11,046 --> 00:01:13,576
NARRATOR: Squeeze the Earth to nine tenths of its size,

39
00:01:13,596 --> 00:01:17,565
NARRATOR: and gravity at the surface grows by almost a quarter.

40
00:01:17,585 --> 00:01:20,098
NARRATOR: The atmosphere thickens.

41
00:01:20,118 --> 00:01:24,139
NARRATOR: Your ears pop, like at the bottom of a swimming pool.

42
00:01:24,159 --> 00:01:26,564
NARRATOR: And somewhere, millions of bathroom scales are

43
00:01:26,584 --> 00:01:30,065
NARRATOR: being blamed for something that isn't their fault.

44
00:01:30,085 --> 00:01:31,810
PIP: Dad would totally blame the scale.

45
00:01:31,830 --> 00:01:34,330
TESS: Dad already blames the scale.

46
00:01:34,350 --> 00:01:36,161
TESS: Then, the days get shorter.

47
00:01:36,181 --> 00:01:37,202
PIP: Shorter how?

48
00:01:37,222 --> 00:01:38,643
TESS: Like a figure skater.

49
00:01:38,663 --> 00:01:40,547
TESS: Arms out, nice and slow.

50
00:01:40,567 --> 00:01:41,644
TESS: Then pull them in!

51
00:01:41,664 --> 00:01:43,638
PIP: Don't you dare spin on the roof!

52
00:01:43,658 --> 00:01:45,016
TESS: Okay, okay!

53
00:01:45,036 --> 00:01:47,128
TESS: But a smaller Earth spins faster.

54
00:01:47,148 --> 00:01:51,355
TESS: At half its size, a whole day would only last six hours.

55
00:01:51,375 --> 00:01:53,582
PIP: So we'd get less time.

56
00:01:53,602 --> 00:01:54,741
TESS: Yeah.

57
00:01:54,761 --> 00:01:56,266
TESS: Less time.

58
00:01:57,224 --> 00:01:59,579
NARRATOR: At half its size, about as big as Mars,

59
00:01:59,599 --> 00:02:02,498
NARRATOR: gravity is four times stronger.

60
00:02:02,518 --> 00:02:03,923
NARRATOR: Just standing up would feel like

61
00:02:03,943 --> 00:02:07,441
NARRATOR: carrying three extra copies of yourself.

62
00:02:07,461 --> 00:02:09,341
NARRATOR: And squeezing anything heats it up,

63
00:02:09,361 --> 00:02:12,520
NARRATOR: like a bicycle pump warming in your hand.

64
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:16,074
NARRATOR: Squeeze an entire planet, and the oceans steam,

65
00:02:16,094 --> 00:02:17,587
NARRATOR: the rocks melt,

66
00:02:17,607 --> 00:02:20,062
NARRATOR: and the ground begins to glow.

67
00:02:20,787 --> 00:02:22,585
PIP: What about people?

68
00:02:22,605 --> 00:02:25,260
TESS: People wouldn't make it this far, Pip.

69
00:02:25,783 --> 00:02:28,613
TESS: So let's watch the rest from somewhere safe.

70
00:02:30,063 --> 00:02:33,141
NARRATOR: From out here, you can finally see it.

71
00:02:33,161 --> 00:02:36,288
NARRATOR: Earth shrinks past the size of the Moon.

72
00:02:36,308 --> 00:02:38,054
NARRATOR: Then a country.

73
00:02:38,074 --> 00:02:39,988
NARRATOR: Then a city.

74
00:02:40,008 --> 00:02:42,163
NARRATOR: Just over a hundred kilometers across,

75
00:02:42,183 --> 00:02:44,718
NARRATOR: it's as dense as a white dwarf,

76
00:02:44,738 --> 00:02:48,441
NARRATOR: the glowing core left behind by a dead star.

77
00:02:48,461 --> 00:02:49,941
NARRATOR: A single teaspoon of it would

78
00:02:49,961 --> 00:02:52,678
NARRATOR: weigh as much as four elephants.

79
00:02:52,698 --> 00:02:53,804
PIP: Four elephants?

80
00:02:53,824 --> 00:02:55,338
TESS: In a teaspoon.

81
00:02:55,358 --> 00:02:59,032
NARRATOR: And a day now lasts about nine seconds.

82
00:02:59,052 --> 00:03:01,945
TESS: Keep going, and it spins so fast it flattens out,

83
00:03:01,965 --> 00:03:04,704
TESS: flinging glowing rock into a ring around itself.

84
00:03:04,724 --> 00:03:05,850
PIP: Like pizza dough!

85
00:03:05,870 --> 00:03:07,950
TESS: Exactly like pizza dough.

86
00:03:08,763 --> 00:03:10,426
NARRATOR: Smaller still.

87
00:03:10,446 --> 00:03:13,572
NARRATOR: Now it's about as wide as a stadium.

88
00:03:13,592 --> 00:03:16,544
NARRATOR: Here, atoms stop being atoms.

89
00:03:16,564 --> 00:03:18,744
NARRATOR: Electrons are crushed into protons,

90
00:03:18,764 --> 00:03:21,908
NARRATOR: and almost everything becomes neutrons.

91
00:03:21,928 --> 00:03:25,333
NARRATOR: Earth is now as dense as a neutron star.

92
00:03:25,353 --> 00:03:28,708
NARRATOR: A teaspoon would weigh about a billion tons.

93
00:03:29,345 --> 00:03:30,453
TESS: Every mountain.

94
00:03:30,473 --> 00:03:31,618
TESS: Every ocean.

95
00:03:31,638 --> 00:03:32,940
TESS: Every city.

96
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:34,615
TESS: In a ball you could walk around

97
00:03:34,635 --> 00:03:36,915
TESS: before your cocoa gets cold.

98
00:03:37,727 --> 00:03:39,470
NARRATOR: And then, when the entire planet is

99
00:03:39,490 --> 00:03:43,105
NARRATOR: squeezed to less than two centimeters across,

100
00:03:43,125 --> 00:03:44,930
TESS: about the size of a marble,

101
00:03:44,950 --> 00:03:47,295
NARRATOR: gravity wins.

102
00:03:47,315 --> 00:03:50,309
NARRATOR: Not even light can escape.

103
00:03:50,329 --> 00:03:53,059
NARRATOR: Earth becomes a black hole.

104
00:03:55,157 --> 00:03:57,830
TESS: We've always called Earth the blue marble.

105
00:03:57,850 --> 00:04:00,130
TESS: It would finally be one.

106
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:02,101
TESS: It just wouldn't be blue.

107
00:04:02,973 --> 00:04:04,466
PIP: And the Moon?

108
00:04:04,486 --> 00:04:06,703
NARRATOR: The Moon, now the size of a peppercorn,

109
00:04:06,723 --> 00:04:07,941
NARRATOR: whips around the black hole

110
00:04:07,961 --> 00:04:10,555
NARRATOR: from about an arm's length away.

111
00:04:10,575 --> 00:04:12,693
NARRATOR: Eight million times every second,

112
00:04:12,713 --> 00:04:15,906
NARRATOR: at nearly a tenth of the speed of light.

113
00:04:15,926 --> 00:04:17,106
NARRATOR: Then it spirals in,

114
00:04:17,126 --> 00:04:19,231
NARRATOR: sending one last chirp of gravitational

115
00:04:19,251 --> 00:04:22,594
NARRATOR: waves rippling across space.

116
00:04:22,614 --> 00:04:25,644
NARRATOR: And then, silence.

117
00:04:28,085 --> 00:04:30,993
NARRATOR: And here's the strangest part.

118
00:04:31,013 --> 00:04:33,509
NARRATOR: Nothing else would notice.

119
00:04:33,529 --> 00:04:38,093
NARRATOR: From far away, gravity only cares about mass, not size.

120
00:04:38,113 --> 00:04:40,989
NARRATOR: The Sun would hold on just the same.

121
00:04:41,009 --> 00:04:43,439
NARRATOR: Mars wouldn't feel a thing.

122
00:04:44,111 --> 00:04:46,335
TESS: It would just keep going around the Sun.

123
00:04:46,355 --> 00:04:47,592
TESS: Once a year.

124
00:04:47,612 --> 00:04:49,515
TESS: Every year.

125
00:04:49,535 --> 00:04:52,165
PIP: Like it's still counting birthdays.

126
00:04:52,756 --> 00:04:55,570
PIP: That's the saddest thing I've ever heard.

127
00:04:55,590 --> 00:04:56,832
TESS: Good news, though.

128
00:04:56,852 --> 00:04:58,123
TESS: It can't happen.

129
00:04:58,143 --> 00:04:59,079
PIP: Why not?

130
00:04:59,099 --> 00:05:01,721
TESS: Earth is way too light to collapse on its own.

131
00:05:01,741 --> 00:05:03,513
TESS: Even the Sun is too light,

132
00:05:03,533 --> 00:05:04,801
TESS: and it's more than three hundred

133
00:05:04,821 --> 00:05:06,838
TESS: thousand times heavier than us.

134
00:05:06,858 --> 00:05:09,865
TESS: Only giant stars ever get crushed like that.

135
00:05:09,885 --> 00:05:12,158
TESS: Everything else pushes back.

136
00:05:12,178 --> 00:05:15,271
NARRATOR: Every atom resists being squeezed.

137
00:05:15,291 --> 00:05:19,799
NARRATOR: The electrons inside refuse to crowd into the same space.

138
00:05:19,819 --> 00:05:22,399
NARRATOR: It's why the ground holds you up.

139
00:05:22,419 --> 00:05:25,474
NARRATOR: It's why you don't sink through your chair.

140
00:05:26,005 --> 00:05:27,572
PIP: So the harder you squeeze it,

141
00:05:27,592 --> 00:05:29,298
PIP: the harder it pushes back?

142
00:05:29,318 --> 00:05:30,400
TESS: Pretty much.

143
00:05:30,420 --> 00:05:32,290
TESS: Stubborn little things.

144
00:05:32,310 --> 00:05:33,422
PIP: Like you.

145
00:05:33,442 --> 00:05:34,972
TESS: Like... us.

146
00:05:36,478 --> 00:05:39,209
PIP: It's still gonna be really far.

147
00:05:39,229 --> 00:05:40,117
TESS: Yeah.

148
00:05:40,137 --> 00:05:41,645
TESS: It is.

149
00:05:41,665 --> 00:05:43,454
TESS: But the Moon looks the same size from

150
00:05:43,474 --> 00:05:46,676
TESS: my dorm as it does from this roof.

151
00:05:46,696 --> 00:05:48,642
TESS: So when things get heavy,

152
00:05:48,662 --> 00:05:49,828
TESS: look up.

153
00:05:49,848 --> 00:05:52,028
TESS: I'll be looking at the same one.

154
00:05:53,270 --> 00:05:57,096
NARRATOR: The Earth will never be squeezed into a marble.

155
00:05:57,116 --> 00:05:59,871
NARRATOR: It's far too stubborn for that.

156
00:06:00,714 --> 00:06:02,799
NARRATOR: And it turns out,

157
00:06:02,819 --> 00:06:04,474
NARRATOR: so are we.
